Issue:
Reuse Kubernetes Client instances
### Problem to solve

As its been discussed in this issue #1244, the StackGres Operator is consuming high leves of CPU due 
  to the constant creation Kubernetes Client instances. 

### Further details

The fabric8 Kubernetes Client uses OkHttp. The OkHttp client from the moment is instantiated creates a pool of http 
  connections, which provides a good performance as long as the same client instance is reused. 

By creating a new Kubernetes Client every time that is needed, is easy to end up creating thousands of short lived threads that 
  increases CPU usage and memory preassure unnecessarly. 

The Kubernetes Client implements Autoclosable interface, which sugest to developers to close the Kubernetes Client once is not 
  needed anymore. Therefore, to reuse the Kuberentes Client we need to be sure that is not going be closed accidentally.

### Proposal

Use the KubernetesClientProvider to create a single instance the Kubernetes Client, then use a Proxy class to prevent closing 
  from happening. 

Lastly, on KubernetesClientProvider Pre destroy phase closing the given Kubernetes Client.

[DISCUSSION]
[xaviersierra]: There is a unexpected issue that I encounter implementing this. 

For most of our modules (operator, jobs, cluster-controller and distributed logs), this solutions works fine. But the REST API have different requirements, which are related to the RBAC. 

This is the current implementation of the current Provider for the REST API. 

``` java
@RequestScoped
public class KubernetesClientProvider implements KubernetesClientFactory {

  private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(KubernetesClientProvider.class);

  @Inject
  @Claim(standard = Claims.sub)
  String impersonate;

  @Override
  public KubernetesClient create() {
    Config config;
    if (null != impersonate) {
      LOGGER.debug("Impersonate user {}", impersonate);
      config = new ConfigBuilder()
          .withImpersonateUsername(impersonate)
          .withImpersonateGroup("system:authenticated")
          .build();
    } else {
      config = new ConfigBuilder().build();
    }

    return new DefaultKubernetesClient(config);
  }

}

```

Basically what this does, is chaning the kubernetes client configuration in order to impersonate an user. Under the hood what fabric8 does is create and http interceptor, as it can be seen [here](https://github.com/fabric8io/kubernetes-client/blob/0f1e4873ba1d2816eeeba67241aa534ea7d4ad51/kubernetes-client/src/main/java/io/fabric8/kubernetes/client/utils/HttpClientUtils.java#L289). 

The problem with this, is that we cannot make a single http request with the impersonated user without affecting all connections in the OkHttpClient pool.

[xaviersierra]: I think we should merge the proxy for the other modules, and then find out a solution for this case.

[teoincontatto]: I agree. Lets merge the part that has been implemented and create a side issue to solve in the future the REST API part of this issue.

